{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And these are the generations of Aaron and Moses, etc.</b> Rashi explained that since Moses taught them Torah, it is as if he gave birth to them, and they became his generations on the day that HaShem spoke with him at Mount Sinai. And it is difficult: if so, then all Israel are his generations, for he taught Torah to all of them. And furthermore, why did he repeat afterward, 'And these are the names of the sons of Aaron,' and did not mention Moses? And it appears to me that since it is stated (Deuteronomy 9:20), 'And with Aaron HaShem was very wrathful to destroy him,' and 'destruction' means nothing other than the annihilation of children, etc., as Rashi explained in parashat Ekev (Deuteronomy 9:20) — and the prayer of Moses helped for half of them — it follows that those two sons whom Moses saved through his prayer are as if he gave birth to them, and they became his generations on the day that HaShem spoke with Moses at Mount Sinai: 'Go, descend,' etc. (Exodus 32:7) — and afterward he prayed for them on the mountain, and HaShem answered him there on the mountain. And when it says, 'These are the generations of Aaron and Moses,' it mentioned Moses on account of Eleazar and Ithamar, whom he saved from death. But what it says afterward — 'These are the names of the sons of Aaron' — and did not mention Moses, it said regarding Nadab and Abihu, for them he did not save from death. And through this, what is mentioned here is resolved: the death of Nadab and Abihu, and the survival of Eleazar and Ithamar." "And regarding what is written, <b>'And they had no sons,'</b> and it is taught in the name of the one who says that they died for the sin that they had no sons (Yevamot 64a) — we have already explained in parashat Shemini (Leviticus 10:11) that in the matter of idolatry such is the measure, that the Holy One, blessed be He, visits the iniquity of fathers upon children, upon the third and upon the fourth generation. And the meaning is: sometimes the Holy One, blessed be He, waits until the second generation, and sometimes until the third or fourth generation; and had they had sons, then for the honor of Aaron the Holy One, blessed be He, would have waited yet another generation or two — but since they had no sons, the matter was forced, to visit the iniquity upon them."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:39:33.893091362Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}